WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
This is launch pad 39, at the Kennedy Space Center!

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.880
This is where all the Apollo missions launched from.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.020
This is where more than 50 Space Shuttle missions launched from.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.340
This pad here, this is the flame trench

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.960
where all the, the steam generated

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.780
and the fire generated by those rockets comes out

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:18.300
uhm

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:18.800
I

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:19.300
I am

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:19.800
just

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:20.300
uh

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.300
astonished to be standing here

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.080
in this place

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.080
and here is something you might not know

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.980
The difficulty with getting to orbit

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.760
is not just the altitude

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.940
it's mainly the speed

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.520
If you want to go to orbit

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.320
Then you have to go 15 thousand miles an hour

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.400
if you're going up to International Space Station height

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.280
15 thousand miles an hour

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.440
That's what most of that fuel goes on

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.840
If you don't have that speed

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.400
then you're going to go up...

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.180
...and you're going to come straight back down

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.100
If you can't go fast enough to dodge the curvature of the Earth

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.720
Then you're just going to come straight back down.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.900
The energy budget isn't just on altitude, it's on speed.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.000
It goes from chemical energy in the rocket

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.160
To kinetic energy as it flies

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:00.900
and goes across

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.700
And then burns it off as heat and friction

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.600
as it plunges back down into the atmosphere.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.120
If you're just falling through the atmosphere

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.220
you don't burn up

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.460
but if you're going 15 thousand miles per hour

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:11.540
plunging into it

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.620
and using it as drag to stop

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.720
then, you get the trouble with burning up on reentry.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.240
So there you go

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.360
the problem with orbit is not altitude

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:20.360
but speed.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.060
And that is something you might not have known.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.400
[Translating these subtitles? Add your name here!]

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.740
And that's that shot gone.

